structure, $31,301.80 (AP, supported).\n Mar: JE-00232 (2026-03-03) Stackline Cloud inv STA-98877, production cloud infrastructure, $31,148.20 (AP, supported) \u2014 recurring run-rate essentially flat vs Feb (-$153.60).\n      JE-00258 (2026-03-14) Stackline Cloud inv SC-M-2203, \"one-time data migration\", $9,800.00 (AP, supported).\n Driver: the entire increase is the $9,800 one-time Stackline data migration invoice; the recurring hosting charge is flat.\n Caveat: I confirmed the ledger entry carries a support flag but was NOT able to open the underlying invoice to confirm (a) the service was actually performed in March, (b) it is genuinely non-recurring, and (c) it is properly classified in 5000 rather than capitalizable/prepaid. Note the invoice number format (SC-M-2203) differs from the vendor's normal STA-##### series; controller should open the document to confirm authenticity and period before accepting this driver. Expect 5000 to revert to ~$31k run-rate in April if it is truly one-time.\n\n2) 6300 Travel: +$6,373.01 (+811%) \u2014 NOT FULLY VERIFIED\n Feb (4 card transactions, all supported): JE-00132 Uber $31.36; JE-00167 Delta Air Lines $420.62; JE-00188 Uber $38.32; JE-00196 Hilton Garden Inn $295.10 = $785.40.\n Mar: I could not retrieve the March transaction detail (tool budget exhausted before the query returned). The $7,158.41 is ~9x the February run-rate and the $6.4k increase cannot be attributed to specific transactions from what I have. This driver DOES NOT HOLD UP as explained \u2014 the controller/preparer must list the March 6300 entries and confirm they are supported, employee-related, and not misclassified (e.g., conference fees that belong in 6320, which dropped to zero in March, or a vendor/AP item coded to travel).\n\n3) 6400 Marketing Programs: +$21,787.86 (+276%) \u2014 NOT VERIFIED\n I could not retrieve either the February or March transaction detail for this account before the tool budget was exhausted. A $21.8k month-over-month increase against a ~$7.9k prior-month base is the largest single flux item and must be tied to specific invoices/campaign commitments (event sponsorship, agency fees, or a prepaid that should sit in 1200 and be amortized). Until the ledger detail and support are reviewed, this driver is UNEXPLAINED and should be treated as an open flux item.\n\nOTHER OBSERVATIONS (below the $5k threshold but relevant to close completeness)\n - 6800 Depreciation: $3,425.00 booked in Feb, $0 in March. This is a recurring monthly entry; its absence indicates the March depreciation entry has not been posted. Not a real flux \u2014 a missing close entry. Recommend posting March depreciation (~$3,425) once the fixed-asset schedule is confirmed.\n - 6900 Bank Fees: $65 in Feb, $0 in March. Likely the March bank fee has not been recorded from the bank statement; check the March bank rec.\n - 6320 Training & Conferences dropped to $0 while Travel spiked \u2014 possible coding shift between the two accounts; review alongside item 2.\n - 6200 Software Subscriptions down $800 (-19%): under threshold; may reflect a subscription that lapsed or was moved to prepaid \u2014 not investigated.\n\nWHAT I DID / DID NOT DO\n - No journal entries were posted, no exceptions were flagged via tool, and no checklist items were updated; this task was analytical only and I was cut off before I could flag the Travel and Marketing items formally.\n - Verified: all 28 US expense account balances for Feb and Mar; ledger detail for 5000 (both months) and 6300 (Feb only).\n - Not verified: 6300 March detail, 6400 Feb and Mar detail, underlying support documents for any entry (including the $9,800 migration invoice).\n\nRECOMMENDED FOLLOW-UP\n 1. Open the support behind JE-00258 (Stackline SC-M-2203, $9,800) and confirm service period and non-recurring nature.\n 2. Pull March 6300 and Feb/Mar 6400 ledger detail; tie each entry >$1k to an invoice/receipt; check for misclassification or items that should be prepaid.\n 3. 